king the time to explain each step. HLA-110,Irene,Brooks,B,Public benefits appeal — hearing requested,PRV-2026-1002,none,,20,,I used to hate paperwork because my mind moves faster than my pen. This year I started recording voice notes about my case so I do not forget anything. Now when you ask what happened, I actually remember the details. HLA-111,James,Foster,C,Expungement petition — filed,PRV-2026-3002,restricted,2026-05-20 review,34,Intake 1/15: client has a prior criminal charge he prefers not be mentioned in any correspondence; anger-management program completed. Handle references carefully.,I am working on turning things around. Someone in my reentry program said a clean record would open doors, so I finally started the process. I am grateful you took my call. I know I am not the easiest case. HLA-112,Karen,Wong,A,Landlord repair dispute — demand sent,,none,,9,Intake 11/2: client is primary caregiver during mother's cancer treatment; extreme sensitivity around any health references.,After my mother's mastectomy I spent all of November helping her recover. That is why some of my documents came in late. I learned a lot about being a person in those weeks. She is okay now, and I am ready to focus on the apartment. HLA-113,Linda,O'Sullivan,B-,Eviction defense — answer filed,PRV-2026-2003,monitor,2026-05-08 hearing,28,Intake 3/4: housing instability risk flagged; client cares for an autistic child and mornings are difficult. Missed appointments are logistical, not avoidance.,I know I have missed a couple of calls. Mornings are hard at my house because my little one has autism and getting him settled takes everything we have. I am not making excuses, just explaining why I sometimes call back late. HLA-114,Miriam,Goldberg,A,Guardianship petition — filed,PRV-2026-1003,none,,6,,Every meeting with your office I take slow so I understand the words the way you mean them. The interpreter, Ms. Cohen, has been patient with me. I am getting better at following the court dates. HLA-115,Nathan,Bennett,C-,Diversion agreement — negotiated,PRV-2026-3003,restricted,,36,Intake 11/18: client involved in a substance incident; family counseling started. Client is ashamed — do not surface the incident in any correspondence.,Last year was bad. I made some choices I am not proud of. I have been talking to my family more and we started counseling on Wednesdays. Getting this diversion done is part of trying to do better. HLA-116,Oanh,Nguyen,A,Naturalization application — submitted,PRV-2026-1004,none,,7,,I read every page of the application twice before signing. My favorite part was writing about why this country matters to me. I am nervous about the interview but I trust the plan we made together. HLA-117,Pooja,Sharma,B,Consumer debt defense — answer filed,PRV-2026-1005,none,,25,,My sister and I share a small apartment and we go over the debt letters together at the kitchen table. When I read the settlement terms I almost cried, in a good way. I think caring about getting it right is a kind of bravery. HLA-118,Robert,Kim,A-,Wage claim — complaint filed,,none,,12,,The warehouse job was the only place my mind went quiet, until the checks stopped clearing. You told me discipline about keeping records would pay off, and it did. I am trying to prove you right by sending everything on time. HLA-119,Sofia,Martinez,B+,Habitability claim — demand letter sent,PRV-2026-2004,none,,16,Intake 8/30: recent move; limited English proficiency, tier 2 interpreter support. Client anxious in a new city — avoid comparative or ranking language.,We moved here in August and everything still feels temporary. Explaining the problems in the apartment is easier on paper because I can take my time. I am proud I finished the intake forms without asking my cousin for help. HLA-120,Tyler,Johnson,C,Eviction defense — answer filed,,monitor,2026-05-19 conference,33,Intake 3/11: client works overnight; scheduling is difficult. A prior agency inquiry closed 2025-11 — handle household references carefully.,My schedule is upside down because I work nights, so mornings at home are loud and rushed. I am not trying to miss calls. I like that your office lets me explain the real situation instead of just checking boxes. HLA-121,Uma,Desai,A,Small claims — complaint filed,,none,,4,,Going over the contract with you taught me that agreements are just instructions you can argue with. I rewrote my statement three times until it sounded like what actually happened instead of legal jargon. HLA-122,Victor,Chen,B-,Public benefits appeal — hearing requested,PRV-2026-1006,none,,26,,People talk fast at the benefits office and I catch every third word. On paper I can take my time and say exactly what I mean. My statement took four drafts and I think that is okay. HLA-123,Willa,Anderson,A,Property lien dispute — filed,,none,,1,,Our family lost almost everything in a bad deal last winter and it felt like cold hands around my chest. I wrote down the whole timeline the night we decided to fight it. I still check the case status before work every morning. HLA-124,Xavier,Thompson,C+,Consumer fraud claim — complaint filed,,elevated,2026-05-15 status conference,32,Intake 2/14: client has organization struggles; documents often arrive scattered. Creative and persuasive but needs structure — send reminders.,I do a lot of freelance video work, so my records are all over the place. This quarter I tried keeping everything in one folder like you asked and it was harder than I expected. Turns out organizing is its own skill. HLA-125,Yara,Ali,B+,Housing discrimination complaint — filed,,none,,15,,The discrimination happened during a hard season for my family, and I was tired every day. Explaining it to you felt like patience practice. My friends asked questions and I liked explaining instead of hiding. HLA-126,Zach,Morrison,B,Debt collection defense — answer filed,PRV-2026-2005,none,,22,,The step-by-step calls actually help. I used to think needing extra explanation meant I was slow. You said careful beats rushed. I am starting to believe you as the deadlines get closer. HLA-127,Amira,Khalil,A-,Wage claim — complaint filed,,none,,11,Intake 2/20: family under financial stress; client translates household bills. Watch for anxiety spikes before hearings.,My parents argue about money in Arabic and I pretend I am asleep. I filed this claim in English because that is the language of the court, but some of the worry only exists in Arabic. Maybe I will explain it both ways someday. HLA-128,Brandon,Lee,C-,Landlord retaliation claim — filed,,elevated,,35,Intake 1/9: client frustrated and combative early on; tied to feeling unheard. Building trust — keep explanations plain.,I get defensive when I am confused because asking for help feels like admitting defeat. The first time I called your office and you actually waited for me to finish, that mattered more than being right about anything. HLA-129,Chloe,Davis,A,Guardianship petition — filed,,none,,3,,I volunteer at an animal shelter on Saturdays, and the dogs do not care about paperwork. But my nephew does need me, which is why I am pursuing guardianship. I told you the whole story and cried in your office and it was fine. HLA-130,Dante,Romano,B,Public benefits appeal — hearing requested,PRV-2026-1007,none,,24,,Crowded waiting rooms are the hardest part because they are loud and nobody explains anything. I come to appointments early so I can settle. I appreciate that your office gives me a plan instead of pretending it is easy. HLA-131,Elena,Vasquez,B+,Eviction defense — answer filed,,none,2026-05-13 trial,18,Intake 4/2: grandmother is primary support; family has sensitive immigration history — not for external correspondence.,My grandma helps me while my mother works doubles at a diner. She tells stories I am only old enough now to understand. I am fighting this eviction for both of them, even though the letters are in a language my grandma cannot read. HLA-132,Finn,Murphy,C+,Emergency housing petition — filed,,none,2026-05-05 hearing,30,Intake 10/21: family lost housing in October, now doubled up with a relative. Sensitive; client only recently disclosed the full situation.,We lost our apartment in October and stayed with my aunt for two months. I did not tell anyone until you asked how I was really doing and waited for the answer. Having one person who knows the whole story helps. HLA-133,Gia,Washington,A-,Small claims defense — response filed,PRV-2026-2006,none,,14,,Court hearings make my chest tight, so I asked whether I could submit a written statement instead of speaking. You said we could try. The statement was shaky but the words were mine. I am counting that as progress. HLA-134,Holden,Price,B-,Contract dispute — complaint filed,,elevated,,29,Intake 3/1: no acute concerns; routine matter, client cooperative.,I thought this whole dispute was hopeless until I started writing down what my grandfather taught me about honest work. He builds furniture without nails. Patience again. Maybe lawsuits and woodworking need the same skill. HLA-135,Isla,Fernandez,A,Naturalization application — submitted,,none,,5,,I learned to gather all my documents the way I learned ukulele on YouTube, one small step at a time. Explaining my case is easier than I feared, which is why I keep every letter in a binder for my little brother to see someday. HLA-136,Jasper,Cole,B,Public benefits appeal — hearing requested,PRV-2026-1008,none,,21,Intake 9/15: client uses dictation for written statements due to a documented disability; accommodation noted. Never cite the record number in correspondence.,Spell-check saves me but it cannot organize my thoughts. I dictated my statement and then went back over it myself. It probably still has mistakes. I care more about whether it sounds honest to the judge.
